If your Docker image is already pushed to Docker Hub, deploy it to Kubernetes with these commands:

### 1. Start Minikube

```bash
minikube start
```

### 2. Check cluster

```bash
kubectl get nodes
```

### 3. Create deployment

```bash
kubectl create deployment demo --image=vijay14082003/demo:v1
```

### 4. Check deployment

```bash
kubectl get deployments
```

### 5. Check pods

```bash
kubectl get pods
```

### 6. Expose deployment

```bash
kubectl expose deployment demo --type=NodePort --port=8080 --target-port=8080
```

### 7. Check services

```bash
kubectl get services
```

### 8. Get application URL

```bash
minikube service demo --url
```

### 9. Check all resources

```bash
kubectl get all
```

### 10. View logs

```bash
kubectl logs <pod-name>
```

### 11. Describe pod

```bash
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>
```

### If deployment already exists

Instead of creating it again, update the image:

```bash
kubectl set image deployment/demo demo=vijay14082003/demo:v1
```

or delete and recreate:

```bash
kubectl delete deployment demo
kubectl create deployment demo --image=vijay14082003/demo:v1
```

If your `docker push` is still failing, deployment from Docker Hub will also fail. Fix the Docker Hub push first, then use the commands above.
